Strawberry Video Player
=======================

A video player, HDMI diagnostic tool and test pattern generator for AV professionals,
for the CardputerZero.

Runs on the CardputerZero (Raspberry Pi CM0, Debian 13 arm64), with no network needed.

  Offline video player:       plays the videos in ~/Videos alphabetically and loops (loop one,
                              loop all). It shows on HDMI when a display is connected,
                              otherwise on the built-in 320x170 LCD, and sound follows the
                              picture.
  HDMI diagnostic tool:       key i shows what the display reports (name, maker, active mode,
                              color range, audio formats, link status, CEC address) on the LCD.
  Test pattern generator:     key t shows color bars, gray steps, geometry, a convergence
                              grid, and an audio and sync test on HDMI.

Keys (Cardputer keyboard, or any attached USB keyboard)
  Tab               swap LCD / HDMI playback
  q, Space, Return  play / pause
  z, x, Del         previous video
  c, f              next video
  w / e             back / forward 30 s
  s / d             volume down / up
  a                 mute
  l                 loop one / loop all
  0 / o             LCD off / on while playing on HDMI (saves a little power)
  p                 subtitles on HDMI: off, then each embedded track
  1-8               display aspect ratio: 16:9, 16:10, 4:3, 5:4, 1:1, 2.35:1, 2.21:1, 21:9
  h                 help (on the LCD while HDMI plays)
  t                 test patterns on HDMI on / off; z x c f choose the pattern: color bars,
                    gray steps, geometry, convergence, audio + sync
  i                 HDMI diagnostics on the LCD: display name, mode, color range, audio,
                    link, CEC address
  Esc               quit

  More about the keys:
  - Previous / next wrap around the list. Loop starts as "loop one".
  - 1-8 set the shape of your display's panel and are remembered (see "Configuration file").
  - t brings the video back where it was when you switch the patterns off, and the patterns
    stay up until you change or switch them off. They also work when there are no videos.
  - The i page also shows for a few seconds whenever HDMI is connected, until playback starts.
    It lists the active mode, color range and bit depth, audio formats, link status, CEC
    address, and the display's native and supported modes.

Output
  HDMI connected at start -> HDMI, sound over HDMI
  otherwise               -> built-in LCD, sound over the onboard speaker
  If HDMI is unplugged during playback the video moves to the LCD at the
  same position.

Usage
  strawberry-video-player              play ~/Videos
  strawberry-video-player DIR          play DIR
  strawberry-video-player FILE         play FILE's folder, starting at FILE

USB drives
  Plug in a USB drive (FAT32, exFAT, NTFS or ext4) and the videos on it play, from
  a folder named Videos on the drive or else from its top level. Remove the drive
  and playback returns to ~/Videos. The player mounts the drive itself (the desktop
  is stopped while HDMI plays), read-only with nosuid, nodev and noexec, under
  /media/strawberry-video-player, using udisks or the stock image's passwordless
  sudo ("sudo -n mount" and "sudo -n umount", nothing else). It unmounts the drive
  when it is removed and when the player quits. Nothing is written to the drive.

How HDMI works
  Preferred: VLC with Raspberry Pi's DRM video output. For files the Pi's hardware
  decoder supports, it feeds the display directly (zero-copy), which is what makes
  1080p smooth on this hardware. VLC needs the display to itself, so the desktop
  (lightdm) is stopped while it plays and started again afterwards (about 10
  seconds to come back). A small watcher restarts the desktop even if the player
  is killed. The LCD shows an info page (title, EDID name, progress, volume) and
  is kept awake while the player runs. There is no on-screen text on HDMI itself.

  This needs permission to stop and start lightdm.service. On the stock
  CardputerZero image nothing needs to be set up: the pi user has passwordless
  sudo, and the player uses it for exactly two commands,
  "sudo -n systemctl stop lightdm" and "sudo -n systemctl start lightdm".
  If your system has neither sudo nor a polkit rule for that, the player plays
  HDMI in a window on the desktop (mpv, software decoding), which is less smooth
  at 1080p, and the LCD shows how to fix it: as an administrator, run

    sudo strawberry-video-player --setup

  That installs a small polkit rule (/usr/share/polkit-1/rules.d/50-strawberry-video-player.rules)
  letting members of the video group stop and start lightdm, and nothing else.
  Undo it with: sudo strawberry-video-player --unsetup

  Hardware or software decoding is chosen per file from its stream header
  (ffprobe). The Pi's hardware decoder handles 8-bit 4:2:0 H.264, profiles up to
  High, at level 4.2 or lower. Anything else (for example a level 5.1 encode with
  10 reference frames, which shows a black or gray picture in hardware) is played
  with the software decoder automatically: this works for 1080p24 at up to about
  20 Mb/s but uses about 250% of the four cores, and switching between a hardware
  file and a software file restarts VLC for a couple of seconds.

  Encoding for the Pi (smoothest, lowest CPU): H.264, High profile or lower, level
  4.1 or 4.2, 4 reference frames, 8-bit 4:2:0, 1080p24-30, up to ~20 Mb/s. With
  ffmpeg:  -c:v libx264 -profile:v high -level 4.1 -refs 4 -pix_fmt yuv420p

  LCD: mpv with software decoding and an early downscale to 320x170, with
  on-screen messages.

Environment variables
  STRAWBERRY_DEBUG=1           log to stderr (player logs in $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR)
  STRAWBERRY_HDMI_BACKEND=     vlc or mpv (desktop window); default: vlc if allowed
  STRAWBERRY_HDMI_VO=          gpu|wlshm renderer for the mpv desktop-window mode
  STRAWBERRY_VLC_ARGS=         extra options appended to the VLC command line (testing)
  STRAWBERRY_HDMI_MODE=        HDMI mode WxH@Hz (default 1920x1080@30: the display's 1080p mode
                               with the nearest refresh rate; many displays offer only 50/60 Hz)

Test patterns (key t, HDMI only)
  1 Color bars (75% bars over an 11-step gray strip)
  2 Gray steps 0, 16, 32 ... 240, 255 plus a ramp: blocks that look identical show crushed
    blacks or clipped whites (a limited/full range mismatch)
  3 Geometry: a 1:1 square and a circle; they must look square and round (keys 1-8 set the
    display's aspect ratio while this is showing)
  4 Convergence: white grid in a red ring of cells, a dot in every cell
  5 Audio + sync: a 30 second 960x540 loop with a 1 kHz tone and a flash on the left, then the
    right, then both; picture and sound should land together
  Note: stills may be sent with a different color range than video. Check the Color line of
  the HDMI diagnostics page (key i) while a pattern is up.

Configuration file (optional): ~/.config/strawberry-video-player.conf
  hdmi_mode = 1920x1080@30     # same as STRAWBERRY_HDMI_MODE
  display_aspect = 16:10       # same as STRAWBERRY_DISPLAY_ASPECT
  Some monitors have a 16:10 (or 4:3) panel and stretch the 1920x1080 signal to fill it, so
  everything looks too tall. Press 1-8 to pick the panel's shape (or set display_aspect):
  the player then letterboxes the video inside the signal and the monitor's stretch makes
  it look right. The choice is saved in this file. Changing it restarts VLC for a few seconds.

Command line
  strawberry-video-player --start 11:40 FILE    start at a position (SECONDS or MM:SS)

Notes
  Videos must be readable by the user running the player.
  Memory: the display shares a CMA pool with the decoder. Raise it with
  dtoverlay=vc4-kms-v3d,cma-128 in /boot/firmware/config.txt (recommended).
